@dimapihtar the two `/claude fix` runs already did their job: the only conflict was in `fused_mla_yarn_rope_apply.py` near the existing stride asserts, and `3f80edc` resolved it correctly (both `cos.shape[-1] == emb_dim` / `sin.shape[-1] == emb_dim` guards are intact).

The remaining `linting` failure is unrelated to that conflict. `tools/check_kernel_determinism_coverage.py` is flagging that `fused_mla_yarn_rope_apply.py` changed without a matching change to `tests/unit_tests/determinism/kernels/test_fused_triton_kernels.py`. This PR only adds two assert statements that reject an already-invalid `cos`/`sin` shape before the kernel launch; for any input that was valid before this PR, nothing about the computed output changes, so it cannot affect numerics. Per `docs/developer/determinism/testing.md`, that is exactly the case the `determinism-exempt` label is for, rather than adding a determinism test.

One snag: that label does not exist on this repo yet (checked via the labels API). `manifest.py`'s own comment on `EXEMPT_LABEL` says a maintainer needs to create it once — could you (or whoever has repo-settings access) add a `determinism-exempt` label and apply it here? `codeowners-approval` and `multi-approval-bot-summary` are just pending-review gates, not failures caused by the diff.

